 # On-disk format
 
-The library file is a JSON object:
+The library file is a JSON object (schema 2):
 
 ```json
 {
-  "schema": 1,
+  "schema": 2,
   "books": [
-    {"id": 1, "title": "Dune", "author": "Frank Herbert", "year": 1965}
+    {"id": 1, "title": "Dune", "author": "Frank Herbert",
+     "published": 1965, "format": "print"}
   ]
 }
 ```
 
-| field    | type   | notes                                  |
-|----------|--------|----------------------------------------|
-| `id`     | int    | positive, unique within the file       |
-| `title`  | string | non-empty                              |
-| `author` | string | non-empty                              |
-| `year`   | int    | publication year, `null` when unknown  |
+| field       | type   | notes                                        |
+|-------------|--------|----------------------------------------------|
+| `id`        | int    | positive, unique within the file             |
+| `title`     | string | non-empty                                    |
+| `author`    | string | non-empty                                    |
+| `published` | int    | publication year, `null` when unknown        |
+| `format`    | string | required: `print`, `ebook` or `audio`        |
 
-`schema` is the format version. Files with any other `schema` value are
-rejected with `StorageError`. Writes go to a temporary file first and are then
-renamed over the target, so a crash never leaves a half-written library.
+`schema` is the format version. `load` accepts schema 1 and 2; any other
+value is rejected with `StorageError`. `save` always writes schema 2.
+
+## Migration from schema 1
+
+Schema 1 records used `year` instead of `published` and had no `format`.
+They are migrated in memory on load: `year` becomes `published` and `format`
+becomes `"print"`. Loading never rewrites the file; it is upgraded the next
+time the library is saved (for example by `add` or `remove`).
+
+Writes go to a temporary file first and are then renamed over the target, so
+a crash never leaves a half-written library.
